        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Decision One: Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle weight is the single spec that most changes how a paddle plays. The industry broadly groups paddles into three bands: lightweight at roughly 7.5 ounces and under, midweight at about 7.6 to 8.2 ounces, and heavyweight at around 8.3 ounces and up. These bands are a common convention among manufacturers and retailers rather than a rule, but they are a useful starting point because most paddles are listed with a weight range that falls into one of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lighter paddles are quicker in hand battles at the kitchen and easier to maneuver for resets, and they ask less of your shoulder over a long session. The trade is power: a light paddle carries less mass through the ball, so you supply more of the swing speed yourself, and players with elbow trouble sometimes find that swinging harder to compensate makes things worse rather than better. Heavier paddles drive the ball deeper with less effort and feel more stable against hard incoming shots, but they are slower to react and more tiring to hold up.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most adult recreational players, a midweight paddle around 7.8 to 8.2 ounces is the sensible default. It is heavy enough to feel solid on drives and blocks, and light enough that it does not drag at the kitchen. If you have a history of tennis elbow or shoulder trouble, the picture is less obvious than "go light": a slightly heavier paddle can absorb more of the impact itself, which is why our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-tennis-elbow/">paddles for tennis elbow</a> guide favours stable, midweight designs over the lightest options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two paddles with the same weight can also feel very different, because balance matters as much as total mass. A head-heavy paddle has a higher swing weight and feels more powerful but slower; a handle-heavy paddle feels quicker. Our full <a href="/pickleball-paddle-weight/">pickleball paddle weight guide</a> explains static weight, swing weight and how lead tape lets you add mass where you want it, which is useful because you can always make a paddle heavier but never lighter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Decision Two: Grip Size and Handle Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grip size is the most overlooked spec on a paddle and one of the most important. Most paddles have a grip circumference between about 4 and 4.5 inches, with 4.25 inches the most common. A grip that is too small makes you squeeze harder to stop the paddle twisting in your hand, and that constant squeeze is a classic route to forearm and elbow soreness. A grip that is too large limits wrist movement, which costs you spin on serves and flicks at the kitchen.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual advice is to size slightly small rather than slightly large, because you can build a thin grip up with an overgrip, but you cannot shave down a thick handle. Each overgrip adds roughly a sixteenth of an inch to the circumference, so a 4-inch handle with one overgrip ends up close to a 4 1/8-inch grip. Our <a href="/pickleball-paddle-grip-size/">pickleball paddle grip size guide</a> covers the ring-finger measuring method and how to judge fit in the shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle length is a separate choice. Standard handles run around 5 inches, and longer handles of roughly 5.5 inches or more suit players who hit two-handed backhands or who came from tennis and like to choke up and down the grip. The cost of a longer handle is a shorter face, because the rules cap the paddle's combined length, so you give up a little hitting surface for the extra grip room.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you play with a two-handed backhand, a longer handle is close to essential, because two hands on a short handle end up stacked awkwardly with the top hand on the paddle face. If you hit one-handed and stay compact at the kitchen, a standard handle gives you more face and a bigger sweet spot. Either way, measure your grip size before you shop rather than guessing, because it narrows the field quickly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="core" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Decision Three: Core Thickness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every modern paddle uses a polypropylene honeycomb core, and the main variable is how thick it is. The two common sizes are about 13 to 14 millimetres and about 16 millimetres. Thinner cores feel livelier and give more pop, because the face flexes more and the ball leaves faster. Thicker cores absorb more of the impact, which makes the paddle feel softer and more controlled, with a larger, more forgiving sweet spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            For players who value control at the kitchen, a 16mm core is the safer choice. Dinks sit down more easily, resets are easier to keep low, and mishits off-centre lose less. For players who like to attack and want the ball to come off quickly on drives and speed-ups, a 14mm core adds pace. Neither is better in absolute terms; they suit different priorities, and many experienced players own one of each.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a beginner or a recreational player, 16mm is usually the better starting point because the extra forgiveness helps while technique is still developing. The difference is covered in depth in our <a href="/14mm-vs-16mm-pickleball-paddle/">14mm vs 16mm pickleball paddle</a> comparison, including how thermoformed construction changes the picture by adding pop to thicker cores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Core material beyond the thickness rarely matters to a recreational buyer. Nomex and aluminium cores exist but are uncommon in current paddles, and polypropylene dominates because it is quieter and more durable. If a paddle you are considering uses something unusual, treat it as a curiosity rather than a reason to buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="face" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Decision Four: Face Material and Texture
          </h2>

          <p>
            The face is the layer the ball actually touches, and it decides feel, spin and durability. The main choices are fiberglass, carbon fiber and various hybrids. Fiberglass faces are typically springier and more powerful, which suits players who want easy depth. Carbon fiber faces are stiffer and tend to give more control and a more consistent response across the face, which is why they dominate at higher price points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw carbon fiber, often called T700 or similar after the grade of carbon used, has become popular because its textured surface grips the ball for spin. Texture wears over time, though, and the amount of grit on a face is limited by the rules governing roughness, so be wary of spin claims that sound too good to be true. A paddle's spin depends on the face surface, the core and your swing; the material alone does not guarantee it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a first serious paddle, a carbon fiber face on a 16mm core is a dependable all-round combination. If you want more power without swinging harder, fiberglass is the honest alternative and often cheaper. Our <a href="/carbon-fiber-vs-fiberglass-pickleball-paddle/">carbon fiber vs fiberglass</a> comparison covers the trade-offs, including how hybrid faces try to split the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Durability is the quiet factor here. Textured faces lose grip as the surface wears, and any paddle can develop dead spots as the core breaks down with heavy use. A face that looks fine can still play differently after a season of regular use, which is worth remembering when you compare a cheap paddle you will replace yearly with a more expensive one you expect to last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Decision Five: Shape and Sweet Spot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle shape is a trade between reach and forgiveness. Standard or widebody paddles are roughly 16 inches long and 8 inches wide, which gives the largest sweet spot and the most forgiveness on off-centre hits. Elongated paddles stretch to around 16.5 inches or more and narrow to about 7.5 inches, adding reach and leverage for drives and serves at the cost of a smaller sweet spot and a slightly slower feel at the kitchen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid shapes sit between the two and are increasingly common, because they offer a little extra reach without the narrow face of a full elongated paddle. If you are unsure, a standard or hybrid shape is the safer pick for most recreational players, because a big sweet spot rescues more imperfect contact than extra reach adds winners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Singles players and those who hit hard from the baseline benefit most from an elongated paddle, because reach matters more when you cover the whole court alone. Doubles players who spend most of their time at the kitchen often prefer a wider face. Our <a href="/elongated-vs-standard-pickleball-paddle/">elongated vs standard paddle</a> guide walks through the dimensions and who each shape suits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever shape you choose, remember that the rules limit the paddle's combined length and width, so a longer face usually means a narrower one or a shorter handle. Manufacturers juggle those three dimensions differently, which is why two paddles described as "elongated" can still feel quite different in the hand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Decisions Six and Seven: Budget, Approval and Play Style
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price follows materials and construction. Entry-level paddles with fiberglass or basic composite faces are widely available for well under a hundred dollars, mid-range carbon fiber paddles often sit in the roughly $80 to $150 range, and premium thermoformed or raw carbon paddles commonly run from about $150 to well over $250. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-pickleball-paddle-cost/">how much a pickleball paddle costs</a> breaks those tiers down and explains what the extra money actually buys.
          </p>

          <p>
            Approval matters if you play in sanctioned tournaments. USA Pickleball maintains a list of approved paddles that meet its specifications for size, surface roughness and other properties, and paddles approved for sanctioned play typically carry a mark to that effect. Check the current list on USA Pickleball's website before you buy for tournament use, because approval is by model and standards have been tightened over time. For recreational play, approval is reassuring but not essential.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, match all of this to how you play. A beginner or casual player is best served by a midweight, 16mm, standard-shape paddle with a carbon or fiberglass face and a grip that fits; our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-beginners/">best pickleball paddles for beginners</a> focuses on exactly that profile. A control-focused doubles player can stay with the same recipe and spend more on a premium face. A power player who drives from the baseline can move to an elongated, 14mm or thermoformed paddle.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can, demo before you buy. Many clubs and shops let players borrow demo paddles, and an hour of play tells you more than any spec sheet. When you cannot demo, buy from a retailer with a fair return policy, and use the order above to rule out the wrong paddles first. With weight, grip, core and face settled, the final choice between two or three good paddles is far less consequential than it feels in the shop.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fit first, features second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Get weight and grip right and almost any well-made paddle will serve you well. Get them wrong and no face material or brand will make the paddle comfortable to play with.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the paddle a pro uses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Professional players choose paddles to suit years of refined technique and very specific playing styles. A paddle that rewards a pro's fast hands and heavy topspin can feel unforgiving to a recreational player. Choose for your own weight, grip and play style first, and treat endorsements as marketing rather than advice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring grip size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many players never check grip circumference and end up squeezing a handle that is too small, which is a common route to forearm soreness. Measure your hand before you buy, size slightly small rather than large, and use an overgrip to fine-tune the fit if the handle feels a touch thin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going as light as possible to protect the arm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lightweight paddles are easy to swing, but players often swing harder to make up for the missing mass, and a light paddle transmits more vibration on hard impacts. For sore elbows, a stable midweight paddle is often kinder than the lightest option on the shelf.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing power over control too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin-core, elongated power paddle can feel exciting in the first few rallies, but it punishes mishits and makes the soft game at the kitchen harder. Most improving players gain more points from control and consistency, so a forgiving 16mm paddle is the better first serious choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to check tournament approval</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you plan to play sanctioned events, a paddle that is not on the USA Pickleball approved list can be ruled out at check-in. Confirm the specific model on the current approved list before you buy, rather than relying on packaging or older approval marks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most important thing when choosing a pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Weight and grip size matter most, because they decide whether the paddle is comfortable to play with for a full session. Choose a weight band that suits your strength and any arm issues, then size the grip to your hand. Core thickness and face material come next and fine-tune the balance between power and control.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight pickleball paddle should a beginner use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most beginners do well with a midweight paddle of roughly 7.6 to 8.2 ounces. It is stable enough to block hard shots and drive the ball deep without a big swing, while staying light enough to move quickly at the kitchen. Very light paddles can encourage overswinging, and heavy paddles tire the arm quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 14mm or 16mm pickleball paddle better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better outright. A 16mm core is softer and more forgiving, which suits beginners and control-focused players at the kitchen. A 14mm core is livelier and gives more pop on drives and speed-ups. If you are unsure, start with 16mm and consider a thinner core once your soft game is consistent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a carbon fiber pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Carbon fiber faces are popular because they tend to offer good control, a consistent response and, on textured faces, decent spin. They are usually more expensive than fiberglass. Fiberglass is springier and gives more easy power, so it remains a sensible choice for players who want depth without swinging harder.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know what grip size I need for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common method is to measure from the crease in the middle of your palm to the tip of your ring finger; that distance roughly matches the grip circumference to look for. Most adults fall between about 4 and 4.5 inches. If you are between sizes, choose the smaller grip and build it up with an overgrip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a USA Pickleball approved paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you play sanctioned tournaments, where paddles must appear on the USA Pickleball approved list. For recreational games at parks and clubs, approval is not required, though most reputable paddles are approved anyway. Check the current list on USA Pickleball's website for the exact model before buying for tournament use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on my first pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-made beginner paddle typically costs somewhere around $50 to $100. That budget buys a stable core, a decent face and a grip that holds up to regular play. Premium paddles above about $150 add refinements that matter more to experienced players, so it is reasonable to start modestly and upgrade once you know your preferences.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

